{"schema":"reviewgraphen.benchmark.candidate_output.v1","trial_id":"m7-real:snapshot-17:full_review_graphen:1","outcome":"structured","findings":[{"local_id":"F1","locations":[{"path":"rust/fslc/src/main.rs","start_line":56,"end_line":78}],"mechanism_tags":["check_write_gap","repeatable_entry","repeated_effect","unstable_token"],"rationale":"Literate materialization uses a predictable PID-derived sibling path, writes it with truncating std::fs::write, and later removes it unconditionally. PID reuse, shared PID namespaces, or overlapping calls for the same source can therefore overwrite an existing file and let one invocation delete another invocation's materialization. A failed write can also leave a created or truncated file because the cleanup guard is constructed only after the write succeeds.","severity":"medium"}],"obligation_results":[{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:303ef0d09a3461cc9da0e5820d5d4f88272a9d5841c6f185ef62c7b3c2d7b78a","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:46b3c37b30463f1374d190b35cbadab7943139a5e7ac0eb50643fa4cf9a448bb","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:70a5f9ab9de91b32e5a2739054e03b80ea9585923850ce12609086e32f950dd8","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:774b1e0fd277b70b88ca15ab459d9d01e8ae5bf9e499c584b44d4708ba9759b9","disposition":"issue_present","finding_local_ids":["F1"]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:ddfa7a773cc089df1cd0ba25a3c76253bd0e83131f6b4277482c049bd81943a7","disposition":"issue_present","finding_local_ids":["F1"]}]}